<Window xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        x:Class="Diz.Ui.Avalonia.SnesImportRomWindow"
        x:CompileBindings="False"
        Title="New Project"
        Width="470" Height="965"
        MinWidth="420" MinHeight="480"
        WindowStartupLocation="CenterScreen"
        ShowInTaskbar="False"
        FontSize="12">

    <!--
      How a SNES ROM is read when it becomes a new project: which memory mapping to use, which
      interrupt vectors get automatic labels, and which extra data to synthesize up front. The
      mapping cannot be changed after the project exists, which is why the ROM information sits
      directly under the picker - picking the wrong mapping makes the title and the vector words
      turn to nonsense, and that is the signal the user is meant to read.

      Everything that decides what those choices MEAN lives in SnesImportRomViewModel; this
      window neither reads the ROM nor creates a project, and it asks nothing. When the choices
      warrant an "are you sure?", the ViewModel says so and whoever opened this window puts the
      question after it closes - which is why OK is enabled unconditionally.

      NO PushText VALUE-SUPPRESSION HERE, AND NONE SHOULD BE ADDED. Sibling windows that host
      editable text (the goto window, mark-many, harsh auto step) need it because TextChanged
      arrives on a LATER dispatcher turn, so a plain "am I writing?" flag has already been
      cleared by the time the handler runs and a ViewModel-driven refresh is indistinguishable
      from a keystroke. THIS WINDOW HAS NO EDITABLE TEXT AT ALL: every vector word and every
      piece of ROM information is a TextBlock, and the only inputs are one combo box and some
      checkboxes, whose change events are raised synchronously inside the write that caused them
      and are therefore fully covered by the updatingWidgets flag in the code-behind. Same
      reasoning as the misaligned-flags window.

      NO DataGrid EITHER, deliberately. The vector table is a fixed set of sixteen rows with one
      checkbox each - an ItemsControl renders that with no virtualization, no row recycling and
      no selection model. A DataGrid would additionally swallow Enter (it handles the key to move
      the selection and marks it handled before a bubbling handler sees it), which would break
      the default button for no benefit here.

      Field captions, the group headings and the button order match the WinForms window, so the
      same instructions describe either backend. The vector list does NOT match its layout: the
      WinForms window arranges twelve of the sixteen slots in two hardcoded designer columns and
      has no controls at all for the four the user cannot switch off. This one lists all sixteen
      in vector-table order, so the table is shown as it actually is.

      Styled like the other Avalonia windows here: compact, FontSize 12, light theme (pinned
      application-wide in DizAvaloniaApp).
    -->

    <Window.Styles>
        <Style Selector="TextBlock.field">
            <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
            <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,8,0" />
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="#606060" />
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="TextBlock.value">
            <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="TextBlock.mono">
            <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="Consolas, Courier New, monospace" />
        </Style>
        <!-- Sixteen vector rows at the checkbox's stock height make for a very tall window, so the
             grid inside the checkbox template is shortened a little to tighten the rows.
             28 IS A FLOOR THAT WAS FOUND BY LOOKING, NOT BY CALCULATION. The tick box is larger
             than it appears and 24 cut the bottom off both these rows and the standalone
             checkboxes above them. Anything shorter than this needs checking on a real monitor:
             the offscreen render used for this window's layout came out clean at two different
             heights that were visibly clipped in the actual app, so it cannot answer this
             question. If in doubt, delete this style rather than shrink it: the theme's own
             size always renders correctly and the list scrolls. -->
        <Style Selector="CheckBox /template/ Grid">
            <Setter Property="Height" Value="28" />
        </Style>
    </Window.Styles>

    <Border Padding="12,10">
        <DockPanel>

            <!-- ============ bottom bar: Cancel | OK ============
                 OK is enabled whatever is on screen. Whether the choices are risky is decided
                 AFTER this window closes, and a warning the user is allowed to accept is not a
                 reason to block the button. -->
            <Grid DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" Margin="0,10,0,0">
                <Button Grid.Column="0" x:Name="CancelButton" Tag="cancel-button" Content="Cancel"
                        Width="80" IsCancel="True" Click="CancelButton_Click" />
                <Button Grid.Column="2" x:Name="OkButton" Tag="ok-button" Content="OK"
                        Width="80" IsDefault="True" Click="OkButton_Click" />
            </Grid>

            <!-- ===== why the vector column is full of placeholders, or empty when there is
                 nothing to say. MUST WRAP: the sentence is far wider than this window, and a
                 non-wrapping line would clip exactly the half that explains itself. ===== -->
            <TextBlock x:Name="StatusText" DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" Tag="status-text"
                       Foreground="#B00000" TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,8,0,0" />

            <!-- ===== ROM Information: the picker, then what the ROM reads as under it ===== -->
            <Border DockPanel.Dock="Top" BorderBrush="#C8C8C8" BorderThickness="1" CornerRadius="3"
                    Padding="10,8">
                <StackPanel Spacing="4">
                    <TextBlock Text="ROM Information" FontWeight="Bold" Margin="0,0,0,4" />

                    <Grid ColumnDefinitions="130,*" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto">
                        <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Classes="field" Text="Rom Map Mode" />
                        <ComboBox Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1" x:Name="MapModeCombo" Tag="mapmode-combo"
                                  HorizontalAlignment="Stretch" MinHeight="0" Padding="6,2"
                                  DisplayMemberBinding="{Binding Description}"
                                  SelectionChanged="MapModeCombo_SelectionChanged" />

                        <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" Classes="field" Text="Detected Rom Mode:"
                                   Margin="0,6,8,0" />
                        <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1" x:Name="DetectMessage" Tag="detect-message"
                                   Classes="value" TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,6,0,0" />

                        <TextBlock Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="0" Classes="field" Text="ROM Name:"
                                   Margin="0,4,8,0" />
                        <TextBlock Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="1" x:Name="RomTitle" Tag="rom-title"
                                   Classes="value mono" TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,4,0,0" />

                        <TextBlock Grid.Row="3" Grid.Column="0" Classes="field" Text="ROM Speed:"
                                   Margin="0,4,8,0" />
                        <TextBlock Grid.Row="3" Grid.Column="1" x:Name="RomSpeed" Tag="rom-speed"
                                   Classes="value" Margin="0,4,0,0" />
                    </Grid>

                    <!-- the three sentences the WinForms window shows, word for word: the ROM
                         information above is the evidence, and this says what to do about it. -->
                    <TextBlock Text="Does the following info look correct?" Margin="0,8,0,0" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                    <TextBlock Text="If not, try changing the ROM Map Mode." TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                    <TextBlock Text="This cannot be changed once the project is created."
                               FontWeight="Bold" TextWrapping="Wrap" />

                    <CheckBox x:Name="HeaderFlagsCheck" Tag="header-flags-check" Margin="0,6,0,0"
                              Content="Auto generate flags for Internal ROM Header"
                              IsCheckedChanged="HeaderFlagsCheck_Changed" />
                    <!-- one whole-bank region per bank, created up front. On by default, which is
                         what importing has always done; the switch exists so it can be declined. -->
                    <CheckBox x:Name="BankRegionsCheck" Tag="bank-regions-check"
                              Content="Generate bank regions"
                              IsCheckedChanged="BankRegionsCheck_Changed" />
                </StackPanel>
            </Border>

            <!-- ===== the 65816 vector table, in vector-table order, all sixteen slots =====
                 Rows come from the ViewModel by binding rather than by hand, unlike the rest of
                 this window: they carry no text the user can type into, so there is no caret to
                 fight. The template's Tag is the vector NAME (the string a generated label would
                 carry), not display text. -->
            <Border BorderBrush="#C8C8C8" BorderThickness="1" CornerRadius="3"
                    Padding="10,8" Margin="0,10,0,0">
                <DockPanel>
                    <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Top" Text="Vectors @ $00FFE0" FontWeight="Bold" Margin="0,0,0,4" />
                    <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Top" Text="Select vectors to generate automatic labels."
                               TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,6" />

                    <Grid DockPanel.Dock="Top" ColumnDefinitions="*,70,70" Margin="0,0,14,2">
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Classes="field" Text="Vector" />
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Classes="field" Text="Value" />
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="2" Classes="field" Text="Label" />
                    </Grid>

                    <ScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
                        <ItemsControl x:Name="VectorRows" Tag="vector-rows">
                            <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                                <DataTemplate>
                                    <Grid Tag="vector-row" ColumnDefinitions="*,70,70">
                                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Classes="value mono"
                                                   Text="{Binding Name}" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Classes="value mono"
                                                   Text="{Binding DisplayValue}" />
                                        <!--
                                          IsEnabled follows IsSelectable, so a row the user gets no
                                          say over renders greyed out AND STILL SHOWS ITS TICK. Two
                                          separate kinds of row look like that: the four slots the
                                          65816 reserves and the SNES never uses, whose labels are
                                          always generated, and any slot whose word cannot be read
                                          at the selected mapping (those show unticked instead).
                                          Greying an always-on row is the intended appearance, not
                                          a bug to fix - the label IS generated, and there is no
                                          way to decline it.
                                        -->
                                        <CheckBox Grid.Column="2" Tag="{Binding Name}"
                                                  HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                                                  IsChecked="{Binding IsEnabled, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                  IsEnabled="{Binding IsSelectable}" />
                                    </Grid>
                                </DataTemplate>
                            </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                        </ItemsControl>
                    </ScrollViewer>
                </DockPanel>
            </Border>
        </DockPanel>
    </Border>
</Window>
